#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180675 :tutorial/content "A partition $P$ of a closed interval $[a,b]$ is a finite sequence of real numbers taken from $[a,b]$ such that\n\n$$\na = x_0 <      x_1<      x_2 <      \\ldots <      x_n = b\\,.\n$$\n\nFor example, given the interval $[0,6],$ one possible partition is \n\n$$\n0 < 2 < 5 < 6.\n$$\n\nWe can express this partition using set notation as\n\n$$\nP=\\{0,2,5,6\\}.\n$$\n\nNote that this partition breaks up the interval $[0,6]$ into the union of the following subintervals:\n\n$$\n[0,2] \\cup [2,5] \\cup [5,6]\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "874ce92b-031e-55f5-92e4-5883ab9f029a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3874 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180706 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the partition $P=\\{0,2,5,6\\}$ of the interval $[a,b]=[0,6].$ Then, we have\n\n$$\na=\\underbrace{0}_{\\large x_0}   <   \\underbrace{2}_{\\large x_1}   <  \\underbrace{5}_{\\large x_2} <  \\underbrace{6}_{\\large x_3} =b.\n$$\n\nThis partition breaks up the interval $[0,6]$ into the union of the following subintervals:\n\n$$\n[0,2] \\cup [2,5] \\cup [5,6]\n$$\n\nThe length of the $i$th subinterval, denoted $\\Delta x_i,$ is simply the difference of the endpoints: \n\n- The length of the first interval $[0,2],$ denoted $\\Delta x_1,$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{x}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-{x}_{0} \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The length of the second interval $[2,5],$ denoted $\\Delta x_2,$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{x}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-{x}_{1} \\\\  & =5-2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The length of the third interval $[5,6],$ denoted $\\Delta x_3,$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{x}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-{x}_{2} \\\\  & =6-5 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}Δ{x}_{i} & =Δ{x}_{1}+Δ{x}_{2}+Δ{x}_{3} \\\\  & =2+3+1 \\\\  & =6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is just the length of the interval $[0, 6].$\n\n**Note:** In general, for any partition $P=\\{x_0,x_1,\\ldots,x_m\\}$ of an interval $[a,b],$ the sum \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^m\\Delta x_i\n$$\n\n is just the length of the interval $[a, b],$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i} & =Δ{x}_{1}+Δ{x}_{2}+Δ{x}_{3}+⋯+Δ{x}_{m} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})+({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})+({x}_{3}-{x}_{2})+⋯+({x}_{m}-{x}_{m-1}) \\\\  & =(\\cancel{{x}_{1}}-{x}_{0})+(\\cancel{{x}_{2}}-\\cancel{{x}_{1}})+(\\cancel{{x}_{3}}-\\cancel{{x}_{2}})+⋯+({x}_{m}-\\cancel{{x}_{m-1}}) \\\\  & =-{x}_{0}+{x}_{m} \\\\  & ={x}_{m}-{x}_{0} \\\\  & =b-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "714f6267-47f5-5ba8-a3e7-3b7ef319e601" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5779 :tutorial/title "Sums Over Partitions"}]}
